.TH BASIC 1
.SH NAME
basic \- UNIX BASIC Interpreter from University of British Columbia
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basic
[
.B \-anrsxt
] [ +n ] [ file ] [ plotfile ] [ args ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B basic
is an ANSI compatible BASIC interpreter that runs under VENIX.
.PP
The following options are available:
.TP
.B \-a
Abort (via IOT trap) on any error.
Not for general use.
.TP
.B \-n
Requires that variable names follow the ANSI standard of a
letter followed by an optional digit.
.TP
.B \-r
Runs the file specified.
If
.RI + n
is specified also, then execution
will start at line
.IR n .
Basic will exit upon completion of the program.
.TP
.B \-s
Causes single precision to be used.
Normally all floating point
numbers are stored in double precision.
With the
.B \-s
option, the
numbers are stored as single precision, but all expressions are
still evaluated in double precision.
This option is most useful
when a large floating point array is being used and there is not
enough storage available for all of it in double precision.
.TP
.B \-t
Is a debugging option that traces the operation of the interpreter.
More
.B \-t
options produce more debugging output.
.TP
.B \-x
Is a debugging option that suppresses the normal buffering of
output.
.TP
.I file
An optional file name that specifies a program that is to be read
(or run if
.B \-r
was specified) before control is passed to the user.
.TP
.I args
Is an optional list of arguments that are passed to the program via
the ARG$ function.
The program may then use the argument
strings as it sees fit.
.PP
.B "BASIC Commands"
.br
The following commands are available to control the running of a
BASIC program or interact with the system:
.TP
\fB!\fIcommand\fR
causes the VENIX command
.I command
to be executed, and control to be returned to
.BR basic .
.TP
\fBAUTO\fR [\fILINE1\fR], [\fILINE2\fR]
causes the program to enter into automatic line numbering mode.
Line numbering starts at
.I LINE1
and increments by
.I LINE2
for
each line entered.
Automatic numbering mode is terminated by
a null line, an attention interrupt, or by any error.
A star `*'
will be printed if an automatically numbered line already exists
in the program.
To delete such a line, enter a line with at least
one space.
.TP
.B BYE
causes
.B basic
to exit.
.TP
.B CATALOG
prints out the user's current file catalog (equivalent to
.BR ls (1)).
.TP
.B CLEAR
equivalent to
.B "CLEAR VARS"
plus
.BR "CLEAR STACK" .
This is also
done automatically as part of the
.B RUN
command.
.TP
.B "CLEAR VARS"
clears the variable symbol table.
.TP
.B "CLEAR STACK"
clears the execution stack.
This is necessary after a ``stack
overflow'' error, if BASIC statements (such as ``print i'') are to
be used.
.TP
\fBDEL\fR [\fILINE1\fR], [\fILINE2\fR]
deletes
.I LINE1
through
.I LINE2
inclusive.
It is also possible to
delete a line by just specifying its line number.
.TP
.B DUMP
displays the contents of the symbol table.
This is mainly for
debugging
.B basic
itself.
.TP
.B EDIT
causes the program in memory to be written out into a scratch
file and the system file editor
.RB ( ed (1))
invoked upon it.
Any
changes made to the program will be reflected in the memory
version upon leaving the editor.
.TP
\fBLIST\fR [\fILINE1\fR], [\fILINE2\fR]
causes the program to be listed from lines
.I LINE1
through
.IR LINE2 .
The default range is the entire program.
.TP
.B LOAD
is the same as the
.B OLD
command.
.TP
\fBMERGE\fR [\fIFILE\fR]
causes the program in
.I FILE
to be merged with the current program
in memory.
Existing lines will be replaced when duplicate
line numbers are found.
.TP
\fBOLD\fR [\fIFILE\fR]
causes the current program to be discarded, and a new program
read in from
.IR FILE .
Execution may then be begun via the
.B RUN
command.
Note that all previous variable names are cleared.
.TP
\fBRENUMBER\fR [\fILINE1\fR], [\fILINE2\fR]
causes the program in memory to be renumbered, starting at
.IR LINE1 ,
with an increment of
.IR LINE2 .
.I LINE1
and
.I LINE2
both
default to 10.
.TP
\fBRUN\fR [\fILINE\fR]
causes the program to start execution.
If
.I LINE
is specified, then
execution starts at that line number.
.TP
\fBSAVE\fR [\fIFILE\fR]
causes the program in memory to be saved in
.IR FILE .
.I FILE
defaults to the last name mentioned in an
.B OLD
or
.B SAVE
command.
.TP
.B SCR
erases the program and then clears the symbol table.
.TP
\fBUNSAVE\fR [\fIFILE\fR]
deletes (unlinks, removes) a file saved via the
.B SAVE
command.
.TP
.B VI
is the same as
.BR EDIT ,
except that the visual editor
.BR vi (1)
is used
instead of
.BR ed .
.PP
.B "BASIC STATEMENTS"
.br
The following BASIC statements may be issued from a stored program
or as a statement typed in immediate execution mode:
.TP
\fBCLOSE #\fIn\fR
closes unit
.IR n .
It is quite legal to close a unit that has not been
opened.
When a program begins execution, unit 0 is opened for
input on standard input, and unit 1 is opened for output on standard
output.
.TP
\fBCHAIN\fR \fI"file"\fR [,\fIline\fR]
replace the current program with the program in the file
\fI"file"\fR,
and start execution at the first line of the program, or at line
.IR line ,
if specified.
Note that the
\fI"file"\fR
must be either a string
expression or a string constant.
.TP
\fBDATA\fR \fIconstant-list\fR
consists of one or more constants separated by commas.
Blanks
or tabs are not modified in DATA statements.
.TP
\fBDEF FN\fR \fIvar\fR(\fIargs\fR) = \fIexpr\fR
causes a defined function
\fB(FN \fIvar\fB)\fR
to be defined with the given
dummy arguments.
When `\fBFN\fR \fIvar\fR(...)' appears in an expression
later, the dummy arguments will have real values assigned to
them and the value of the expression will be returned.
.TP
\fBDIM\fR \fIvar\fR(\fIbounds\fR), ...
The dimensions of each of the named variables are defined by
the bounds provided.
Basic allows up to two dimensions for
each vector variable.
Note that a dimensioned variable is distinct
from the scalar variable with the same name.
(For example,
.I a
and
.I a(i)
are different, for all values of
.IR i .)
.TP
.B END
causes the program to terminate.
Some BASIC versions require
that
.B END
only appear on the last line of the program, but this
restriction does not apply to VENIX
.BR basic .
.TP
\fBFOR\fR \fIvar\fR = \fIexpr1\fR \fBTO\fR \fIexpr2\fR [\fBSTEP\fR \fIexpr3\fR]
The variable specified is assigned the value of
.IR expr1 .
The values
.I expr2
and
.I expr3
(if specified) are evaluated and saved.
The following statements up to the matching
.B NEXT
are executed until
the value of
.I var
is greater than
.IR expr2 .
When the
.B NEXT
statement is executed, the value of
.I expr3
is added to
.IR var .
.I expr3
defaults to 1, and may be negative.
If
.I expr3
is negative, the
.B FOR
terminates when
.I var
is less than
.IR expr2 .
.TP
\fBGOSUB\fR \fIline\fR
causes execution to transfer to the line number specified.
When
a
.B RETURN
is executed, control will continue from the next
statement after
.BR GOSUB .
.TP
\fBGOTO\fR \fIline\fR
causes execution to transfer to the line number specified.
.TP
\fBIF\fR \fIexpr\fR \fBTHEN\fR \fIstatement\fR
If the expression
.I expr
is TRUE (non-zero), then
.I statement
will be
executed.
Some versions of BASIC only allow a statement
number or a
.B GOTO
for
.IR statement ,
but here any legal statement
is allowed, including another
.B IF
statement.
Other variations of
the
.B IF
statement are supported, where
.B THEN
is optional, and
.I statement
is a line number.
.TP
\fBINPUT\fR [#\fIn\fR,] [\fI"string"\fR,] \fIvar\fR, ...
The variable (or variables) specified are read from the specified
unit.
If no unit is indicated, unit 0 is used.
If the string is
specified, it will be used as a prompt, as if printed via a
.B PRINT
statement.
.TP
\fBLET\fR \fIvar\fR = \fIexpr\fR
assigns the value of
.I expr
to the variable
.IR var .
Note that
.B LET
is
optional, but should be specified for compatibility with other
BASIC's.
.TP
\fBNEXT\fR [\fIvar\fR]
terminates the preceding
.B FOR
statement.
The value of the
.B FOR
variable is incremented and if not greater than the limit, the execution
continues at the statement following the
.B FOR
statement.
.TP
.B NOTRACE
disables the tracing started by the
.B TRACE
statement.
.TP
\fBON\fR \fIexpr\fR \fBGOSUB\fR \fIline\fR, ...
The value of
.I expr
is taken as an index into the list of line
numbers and transfer is made to the n-th line number specified.
.TP
\fBOPEN\fR \fIfile\fR \fBFOR\fR [\fIINPUT/OUTPUT/APPEND\fR] #\fIn\fR
opens the file
.I file
for either input or output on unit
.IR n .
.I file
may
be any string expression.
The
.I INPUT
statement reads (by
default) from unit 0, the
.B PRINT
statement writes by default to
unit 1.
.I APPEND
is the same as
.IR OUTPUT ,
but writes at the end
of the file.
.TP
\fBPRINT\fR [#\fIn\fR,] \fIexpr\fR [,] [;] ...
The value of the expression is printed on the specified unit.
If
no unit is specified, then unit 1 is used.
It may be either a
number or a character string.
If the comma is specified as a
delimiter, the values will be aligned in fields 16 columns wide.
If
a semi-colon is used, there will be no spacing between fields.
If
there is no delimiter, the last value will be followed by a new
line.
A
.B PRINT
statement by itself will issue a
.IR newline .
.TP
\fBPRINT\fR [#\fIn\fR,] \fBUSING\fR \fIfmt\fR; \fIexpr\fR [,] [;] ...
The value of the expression is printed in the same fashion as the
.B PRINT
statement, except that the arithmetic expression is written
according to the format specification
.IR fmt .
See the subsection
``PRINT USING Format Specifications.''
.TP
\fBREAD\fR \fIvar\fR ...
will read values (from subsequent
.B DATA
statements) into the
variables given.
An error will occur if there are insufficient
values in
.B DATA
statements.
.TP
.B REM
causes the following characters to be taken as a remark or comment.
Note that the same effect can be obtained by starting a
line with either a single or double quote.
.TP
.B RESTORE
causes the next
.B READ
statement to start at the first
.B DATA
statement in the program.
.TP
.B RETURN
causes control to be transferred to the statement following the
last
.B GOSUB
or
\fBON\fR \fIexpr\fR \fBGOSUB\fR
statement.
If there is no
enclosing
.BR GOSUB ,
an error will occur.
.TP
.B STOP
causes the program to terminate with an appropriate comment.
.TP
.B TRACE
causes the line number of each statement to be printed as it is
executed.
This is sometimes helpful in following the execution
of a program that is not behaving properly.
.PP
.B Variables
.br
In ANSI BASIC, a variable must start with a letter, and may have an
optional digit following.
In VENIX
.BR basic ,
only the first two characters
of a variable name are considered significant; an arbitrarily long name
may be used.
The second character does not have to be a digit.
.PP
Following the name, either a
.B %
(percent) or
.B $
(dollar) sign may be
present.
The percent sign indicates that the variable is to hold only
integer values.
The dollar sign indicates that the variable is to hold
string values.
If neither is present, then the variable will hold floating
point values.
Real variables are normally double precision unless the
.B \-s
option is specified.
.PP
.B Functions
.br
The following standard functions are provided:
.PP
Those marked with an asterisk (*) are not ANSI BASIC and are a local
implementation to facilitate communications with the VENIX operating
system.
.TP
\fBABS(\fIvalue\fB)\fR
returns the absolute value of
.IR value .
.TP
\fBARG$(\fIn\fB)\fR *
returns the
.IR n -th
argument specified when
.B basic
was opened.
Argument zero is the name of the program file (if any).
A null
string will be returned if an argument is requested that was not
specified.
This is useful when the
.B \-r
option is used to run a previously
stored program.
.TP
\fBATN(\fIvalue\fB)\fR
returns the arc-tangent (in radians) function of
.IR value .
.TP
\fBCHR$(\fIn\fB)\fR
returns the ASCII character that corresponds to
.IR n .
.I n
should
normally be an integer in the range 0 \(<= n \(<= 127.
Values in the
range 128 \(<= n \(<= 255 are possible.
All other values are taken
modulo 256.
.TP
\fBCOS(\fIangle\fB)\fR
returns the cosine function of
.I angle
in radians.
.TP
\fBDATE$()\fR *
returns the current date in the format yy/mm/dd.
.TP
\fBEXP(\fIvalue\fB)\fR
returns the natural anti-logarithm of
.IR value .
.TP
\fBINT(\fIvalue\fB)\fR
returns the integer value of
.IR value .
.TP
\fBLEFT$(\fIstring, length\fB)\fR
the first
.I length
characters of
.I string
are returned.
It is an error if
there are not
.I length
characters in
.IR string .
.TP
\fBLOG(\fIvalue\fB)\fR
returns the natural logarithm of
.IR value .
.TP
\fBMID$(\fIstring,start, length\fB)\fR
The middle
.I length
characters of
.I string
starting at offset
.I start
(1
= first character) are returned.
It is an error if there are not
.I length
characters in
.I string
after
.IR start .
.TP
\fBRIGHT$(\fIstring, length\fB)\fR
The last
.I length
characters of
.I string
are returned.
It is an error if
there are not
.I length
characters in
.IR string .
.TP
\fBSIN(\fIangle\fB)\fR
returns the sine function of
.I angle
in radians.
.TP
\fBSQR(\fIvalue\fB)\fR
returns the square root of
.IR value .
.TP
\fBSYS(\fIn\fB)\fR *
executes system function
.IR n ,
as follows:
.RS
.nf
.ta 1.5i 2i
	1	turn off input echoing
	2	turn on input echoing
	3	get one character from keyboard
	4	print symbol table
	5	not implemented
	6	not implemented
	7	exit from \fBbasic\fP interpreter
.fi
.RE
.TP
\fBSYSTEM(\fI"string"\fB)\fR *
executes
\fI"string"\fR
as a system (VENIX) command, and returns its
exit status.
Zero means the command terminated properly.
.TP
\fBTAN(\fIangle\fB)\fR
returns the tangent function of
.I angle
in radians.
.TP
\fBTIME$()\fR
returns the current time in hh:mm:ss format.
.PP
.B OPERATORS
.br
The following operators are available:
.TP
+
adds two numeric operands together.
Concatenates two strings.
.TP
\-
subtraction.
.TP
*
multiplication.
.TP
/
division.
.TP
^
exponentiation.
.TP
<>
not equal comparison.
.TP
=
equal comparison, also assignment operator.
.TP
<
less than comparison.
.TP
>
greater than comparison.
.TP
<=
less than or equal comparison.
.TP
>=
greater than or equal comparison.
.TP
.B AND
logical
.B AND
of two comparisons.
.TP
.B OR
logical
.B OR
of two comparisons.
.PP
.B "PRINT USING Format Specifications"
.br
The
.B "PRINT USING"
statement format has the following general format
([ and ] enclose optional features):
.PP
[+][**][$$]###[,]###[,]###.##[+]
.PP
The characters have the following meanings:
.TP
.B #
indicates that a digit may be placed there.
.TP
.B \&.
indicates a decimal point.
.TP
+
indicates a sign may be placed before (or after) the number.
.TP
.B **
causes leading zeros to be replaced with asterisk fill.
(Normally
blanks are used).
.TP
.B $$
causes a dollar sign to be placed at the start of the number.
.TP
.B ,
A comma is inserted into the number if any digits have been
printed.
Unlike some versions of BASIC, the position of the
comma is significant.
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1.4i
/usr/bin/basic	\fBbasic\fP interpreter
/bin/ed	\fBed\fP command editor
/bin/vi	\fBvi\fP command editor
/tmp/Ba*	\fBed\fP or \fBvi\fP temporary files
.fi
.SH BUGS
The
.B MAT
operation is not implemented.
.br
The
.B "PRINT USING"
implements only a minimal subset of the normal
facilities.
.sp
